  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nYesterdays cold front has left us with quite cool temperatures \ntoday, as many locations are only in the 40s as of early this \nafternoon. The surface ridge axis is now shifting through eastern \nND, quickly propagating to the east. This has resulted in sunny\nskies area wide. These clear skies will last into the \novernight, bringing widespread low temperatures in the 30s. The\ncoldest areas will be in portions of west central MN, where a \nfreeze warning has been issued for temperatures as low as 30 \ndegrees. \n\nOver the weekend, upper level ridging will build in. In the low \nlevels, a much warmer airmass will advect in from the west, leading \nto above average temperatures. Highs will be in the upper 50s \nto mid 60s over the weekend. Temperatures of these values (or \neven slightly warmer by Wednesday) will persist all the way into\nthe latter portions of next week. The NBM shows high \npredictability in this long stretch of dry/warm weather, with \nonly a 2 to 4 degree high temperature spread between the 25th \nand 75th percentiles each day. This agreement makes sense \nconceptually due to a blocking pattern setting up with ridging \nover the central US and troughs/lows more or less over each of \nCONUS's coasts. There are signs late in the week of a transition\nto southwesterly flow as the western trough digs and starts to \npropagate eastward. However, how this evolves remains uncertain,\nso at this timeframe the forecast features a smattering of low \nend precipitation chances starting Friday. \n\n...Freeze Tonight...\n\nThe coldest temperatures tonight are forecasted to be in west \ncentral MN, which will be in the closest proximity to the \nsoutheastward propagating surface ridge. Elsewhere winds pick up \nquite rapidly after sunset, increasing mixing, likely keeping us a \ncouple of degrees warmer then we otherwise would be. Due to\nhigher winds across the rest of the FA, temperatures, while\nchilly, should remain above freezing in the mid 30s.",
